UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 4, Week 1, Lesson 2: Painting with warm and cool colours', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Painting with warm and cool colours means planning a palette before you start. Mix warm colours on one side of the palette and cool colours on the other so they do not muddy each other. Lay large areas with flat strokes first, then add details with the tip of the brush. Rinse between warm and cool mixes. Cover the table, wear a smock and wash brushes carefully. Light areas first, darker accents last.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same arts idea to a new artwork or performance, then compare two choices for clarity of effect.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nYou are painting a garden at dawn with a warm pink sky and cool green plants. Plan which colours to mix, which strokes to use and the painting order.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Mix pink and pale orange for sky; mix two greens for plants. Sky with wide flat strokes first, then plant tip strokes, then dark accents.'' Order and separate mixes keep colours clear.\n\nWATCH FOR\nMixing warm and cool paints in the same puddle without planning. That often makes dull brown, so keep families apart until you choose a controlled mix.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nPaint or crayon a scene that uses a warm area and a cool area. On the back write your palette recipes and which stroke made which part. If you have no paint, use coloured pencils in warm and cool groups.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one artistic choice you changed and why. Movement or instrument work is optional, adult-supervised and can be described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Creative Arts","learning_intention":"Painting with warm and cool colours means planning a palette before you start. Mix warm colours on one side of the palette and cool colours on the other so they do not muddy each other. Lay large areas with flat strokes first, then add details with the tip of the brush. Rinse between warm and cool mixes. Cover the table, wear a smock and wash brushes carefully. Light areas first, darker accents last.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Create and perform","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: scrap paper, crayons, safe classroom instruments or clear movement space with an adult nearby"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["CA2-VIS-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Paint or crayon a scene that uses a warm area and a cool area. On the back write your palette recipes and which stroke made which part. If you have no paint, use coloured pencils in warm and cool groups. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using warm palette on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Mixing warm and cool paints in the same puddle without planning.. Correction: Strong answer: ''Mix pink and pale orange for sky; mix two greens for plants.. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Mixing warm and cool paints in the same puddle without planning. That often makes dull brown, so keep families apart until you choose a controlled mix. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating warm palette on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with warm palette, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about warm palette with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for warm palette. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for warm palette. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Mixing warm and cool paints in the same puddle without planning.","A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material.","A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson.","''Mix pink and pale orange for sky;"],"question":"You are painting a garden at dawn with a warm pink sky and cool green plants. Plan which colours to mix, which strokes to use and the painting order. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for warm palette; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Careful use of cool palette and explain why it matters with clear evidence.","Mixing warm and cool paints in the same puddle without planning.","Painting with warm and cool colours means planning a palette before you start.","''Mix pink and pale orange for sky;"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with warm palette?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named cool palette and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name cool palette and explain why it matters only, without linking it to warm palette.","Apply