ice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name the artistic choice, its effect, and keep cultural protocols respectful.","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete.","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster.","Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue."],"question":"Which action keeps elements, techniques and audience effect accurate for warm palette?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Mixing warm and cool paints in the same puddle without planning.","Name cool palette and explain why it matters only, with no linked example.","Paint or crayon a scene that uses a warm area and a cool area.","Retype the worked example with no new example."],"question":"Which independent response best applies warm palette to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about warm palette with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating warm palette on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using warm palette on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for warm palette. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with warm palette, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Mixing warm and cool paints in the same puddle without planning.. Correction: Strong answer: ''Mix pink and pale orange for sky; mix two greens for plants.. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent artwork or performance notes and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-arts-y4-w01-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA Creative Arts alignment using catalogue outcome codes (CA2-*); content-point mapping remains pending. First Nations arts content requires local Aboriginal educator or Custodian guidance before acceptance; do not invent sacred designs.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise warm palette.","Read the mission before you start making or performing.","Keep the audience effect of your choice in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not name an effect clearly."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the element or technique that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore elements, safety or respectful practice.","Prefer a named artistic reason over a guess that merely looks colourful."],"submit":["Confirm that your choice and reason are both present.","Proofread element names and safety notes.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact element, technique or cue before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key artistic choices.","Say what each choice does for the artwork or audience.","A final praise word alone is weaker than a named effect."],"teach2":["Compare a weak choice with a stronger one.","Change the element or technique, not only the decoration.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh subject or piece that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original work notes and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill on new material."]},"subject":"Visual Arts","success_criteria":["I can use warm palette with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify cool palette and explain why it matters.","I can practise brush stroke independently on a new prompt.","I can notice mixing on a palette needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Painting with warm and cool colours means planning a palette before you start.","options":["A guess with no artistic reason.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","Painting with warm and cool colours means planning a palette before you start.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about warm palette with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: Jin paints a sunset beach. She mixes orange and pink for the sky (warm) and blue-green for the sea (cool). Wide flat strokes fill the sky, then a thin cool tip stroke marks the horizon. She keeps the mixes separate so the sunset stays bright.\n\nTRY FIRST\nYou are painting a garden at dawn with a warm pink sky and cool green plants. Plan which colours to mix, which strokes to use and the painting order.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Mix pink and pale orange for sky; mix two greens for plants. Sky with wide flat strokes first, then plant tip strokes, then dark accents.'' Order and separate mixes keep colours clear.","explain":"Painting with warm and cool colours means planning a palette before you start. Mix warm colours on one side of the palette and cool colours on the other so they do not muddy each other. Lay large areas with flat strokes first, then add details with the tip of the brush. Rinse between warm and cool mixes. Cover the table, wear a smock and wash brushes carefully. Light areas first, darker accents last.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Mixing warm and cool paints in the same puddle without planning. That often makes dull brown, so keep families apart until you choose a controlled mix. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Separate warm and cool mixes.","caption":"Keep families apart.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Warm side</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">palette left</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Cool side</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">palette right</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example: Jin paints a sunset beach. She mixes orange and pink for the sky (warm) and blue-green for the sea (cool). Wide Then write your own artistic reason.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.\nJuno: Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Example: Jin paints a sunset beach. She mixes orange and pink for the sky (warm) and blue-green for the sea (cool). Wide Then write your own artistic reason."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect. Apply the lesson idea with evidence. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason.","Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates."],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about warm palette with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: Jin paints a sunset beach. She mixes orange and pink for the sky (warm) and blue-green for the sea (cool). Wide flat strokes fill the sky, then a thin cool tip stroke marks the horizon. She keeps the mixes separate so the sunset stays bright. Look at each line, sound, movement or role choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key artistic choices from the example and say what each one does for the audience or artwork.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful response names the element or technique and its effect, not only “it looks nice”.","explain":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final praise word without naming a choice does not show arts thinking. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Show the artistic choice","visual":[{"alt":"Stroke types in a landscape.","caption":"Big areas then details.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Flat stroke</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">sky fill</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Tip stroke</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">horizon line</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison.","options":["A guess with no artistic reason.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about warm palette with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nMixing warm and cool paints in the same puddle without planning. That often makes dull brown, so keep families apart until you choose a controlled mix. Here is a more helpful example: Example: Jin paints a sunset beach. She mixes orange and pink for the sky (warm) and blue-green for the sea (cool). Wide flat strokes fill the sky, then a thin cool tip stroke marks the horizon. She keeps the mixes separate so the sunset stays bright.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice weakens the work, then write a stronger choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Mix pink and pale orange for sky; mix two greens for plants. Sky with wide flat strokes first, then plant tip strokes, then dark accents.'' Order and separate mixes keep colours clear.","explain":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the decoration without changing the artistic idea does not fix the error. Mixing warm and cool paints in the same puddle without planning. That often makes dull brown, so keep families apart until you choose a controlled mix. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example.","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example.","A guess with no artistic reason.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named."],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about warm palette with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nPainting with warm and cool colours means planning a palette before you start. Mix warm colours on one side of the palette and cool colours on the other so they do not muddy each other. Lay large areas with flat strokes first, then add details with the tip of the brush. Rinse between warm and cool mixes. Cover the table, wear a smock and wash brushes carefully. Light areas first, darker accents last. Now choose a different subject or piece to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same arts idea as the model, but with different content or material.","explain":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new artwork or performance. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Transfer to a new artwork or performance"}],"title":"Year 4, Week 1, Lesson 2: Painting with warm and cool colours","unit_number":1,"unit_title":"Painting, colour and mood","week":1,"week_in_unit":1,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample: Jin paints a sunset beach. She mixes orange and pink for the sky (warm) and blue-green for the sea (cool). Wide flat strokes fill the sky, then a thin cool tip stroke marks the horizon. She keeps the mixes separate so the sunset stays bright.\n\nWrite what makes this example effective, naming the element, technique or performance choice and its effect.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Separate warm and cool mixes.","caption":"Keep families apart.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Warm side</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">palette left</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Cool side</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">palette right</t